  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Consider two polynomials $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$. Prove each of the following. Assume that every polynomial whose degree is taken is nonzero, and that $Q$ is nonconstant in part (c).",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p3-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\deg(P+Q)\\le\\max\\{\\deg(P(x)),\\deg(Q)\\}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\deg(P+Q)\\le\\max\\{\\deg(P),\\deg(Q)\\}$.\n\nAdding cannot introduce a higher power; cancellation may lower the degree.",
          "worked": "Let $\\deg(P)=n$ and $\\deg(Q)=m$. Neither polynomial has a term of degree greater than $\\max\\{n,m\\}$, so their sum has no such term. If the leading terms cancel, its degree decreases. Hence\n\n$$\\deg(P+Q)\\le\\max\\{n,m\\}=\\max\\{\\deg(P),\\deg(Q)\\}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\deg(P\\cdot Q)=\\deg(P(x))+\\deg(Q)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\deg(P\\cdot Q)=\\deg(P)+\\deg(Q)$.\n\nThe nonzero leading terms multiply to give degree $n+m$.",
          "worked": "Let the leading terms of $P$ and $Q$ be $a_nx^n$ and $b_mx^m$, where $a_n,b_m\\ne0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(a_nx^n)(b_mx^m)&=a_nb_mx^{n+m}\\end{align*}$$\n\nEvery other product has lower degree, and $a_nb_m\\ne0$. Therefore\n\n$$\\deg(P\\cdot Q)=n+m=\\deg(P)+\\deg(Q).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\deg(P\\circ Q)=\\deg(P(x))\\deg(Q)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\deg(P\\circ Q)=\\deg(P)\\deg(Q)$.\n\nFor nonconstant $Q$, the leading term of $P(Q(x))$ is $a_nb_m^nx^{nm}$.",
          "worked": "Let $P$ have degree $n$ and leading coefficient $a_n\\ne0$, and let $Q$ have degree $m\\ge1$ and leading coefficient $b_m\\ne0$. If $n=0$, the composition is the same nonzero constant, so its degree is $0=nm$.\n\nFor $n\\ge1$, the leading contribution to $P(Q(x))$ is\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a_n(b_mx^m)^n&=a_nb_m^nx^{nm}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nAll lower powers of $Q(x)$ have degree less than $nm$, and $a_nb_m^n\\ne0$. Hence\n\n$$\\deg(P\\circ Q)=nm=\\deg(P)\\deg(Q).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      }
    ]
  },
